diagnosis_text:
I focused on Location C’s 192.168.200.0/24 path: PC11–PC12 on switch5 and PC13–PC14 on switch6, both uplinking to switch4, then to router4’s eth0 (the 192.168.200.1 gateway). The gateway interface and the L2 path are healthy.

Evidence:
- From each of PC11–PC14, short-interval pings to 192.168.200.1 returned 0% loss with sub‑millisecond RTTs (several runs). Router4’s eth0 ARP table shows all four hosts REACHABLE; a host’s ARP cache shows the gateway’s MAC consistently as fe:47:c8:68:09:ab.
- Router4 eth0 is UP/LINK-DET 10G/full, with zero errors/drops (ip -s link, ethtool, tc qdisc stats). CPU is idle and iptables is open (no rate‑limit). dmesg shows no link up/down events on eth0 during the observation period.
- Switch4/5/6 bridges show stable FDB entries, with router4’s MAC learned only on the correct uplink ports and no evidence of MAC flapping. No STP topology changes are occurring.

Mechanism-wise, if the router port were “dropping and picking back up,” we would see eth0 link flaps, rising errors/drops, FDB churn, or ARP oscillation across the three switches; none are present. The symptom could have been transient or application-side. Root cause: not reproducible; no device or interface fault found on the 192.168.200.1 gateway path.

Corrective fix: none required now. If hiccups recur, enable STP on switch4/5/6 (with portfast on PC ports) as best practice, and add a continuous monitor (e.g., fping/arpwatch) on router4 eth0 to capture any brief link or ARP anomalies for forensic analysis. 
=== END ===